  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Font Loading**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dynamically loading the \"Jameel Noori Nastaliq\" font in my Jetpack Compose Android application. Since this particular font isn\'t available locally or through standard Google Fonts, I\'m exploring methods to handle external `.ttf` files effectively. I\'m taking a close look at how to ensure the font renders correctly across different devices and configurations.\n\n\n**Exploring Font Alternatives**\n\nI\'ve hit a snag, realizing Jetpack Compose doesn\'t use CSS or `@font-face`. Instead, I\'m now evaluating alternatives. \"Noto Nastaliq Urdu\" from Google Fonts seems like a promising near-match, which simplifies things. However, the user specifically requested \"Jameel Noori Nastaleeq.\" If dynamic download is too complex, I\'ll explore offline solutions.\n\n\n**Investigating Downloadable Fonts**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into Android\'s `DownloadableFonts` feature within Jetpack Compose, aiming for dynamic font loading. I\'ve added the necessary dependencies and am focusing on how to integrate a custom font provider, or a download link for the \"Jameel Noori Nastaleeq\" font, into the global theme configuration. Considering the user\'s specific request for that font, I\'m trying to determine the flexibility on font choice. My immediate task is to determine if I can use Google\'s font provider as a workaround or if I must use a download.\n\n\n**Evaluating Font Download Options**\n\nI\'ve realized that a direct URL-based `.ttf` load isn\'t possible in Jetpack Compose, but a local download is permitted. I can use the Google Fonts provider for \"Noto Nastaliq Urdu\", which is a decent option, or use a script to download a font on `npx`. Since \"Jameel Noori Nastaleeq\" isn\'t on Google Fonts, I must download the font locally if it is absolutely necessary. I\'ll focus on creating a Node.js script.\n\n\n**Clarifying Tech Requirements**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the user\'s requirements and the available Android Jetpack Compose options for font loading. I see the confusion about CSS and `@font-face` rules. Compose is different from web frameworks, as it\'s written in pure Kotlin. I am thinking about how to explain that the user\'s preferred approach, based on CSS-like rules, is not available. My current plan is to suggest downloading \"Noto Nastaliq Urdu\" from Google Fonts as the simplest method, or implementing a dynamic font loader in Kotlin using `java.net.URL`.\n\n\n**Revisiting Technical Approaches**\n\nI\'m now refining the loading strategy, recognizing that previous instructions leaned toward a web context, which is not applicable here. Given that I\'m working with an Android Compose app, a web font strategy isn\'t applicable. I am prioritizing finding the \"Jameel Noori Nastaleeq\" font, or an equivalent Nastaliq font, that can be integrated within the Android framework. I am now looking at the `build.gradle.kts` file and `libs.versions.toml` to try adding a dependency. I will likely add `implementation(\"androidx.compose.ui:ui-text-google-fonts:x.y.z\")` to fetch Google Fonts.\n\n\n**Refining Download Strategies**\n\nI\'m now comparing font download options. I see that I can download a `.ttf` file using a Node.js script. Downloading the font with a `Coroutine` in Kotlin and using `Typeface.createFromFile` or `createFromAsset` seems feasible. I\'ll need to define the `FontFamily` with `AndroidFont` in Compose to make it work. I\'m exploring an `npx node` script for the download, if a reliable URL can be found.\n\n\n"
