  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Mapping Urdu Fonts**\n\nI\'ve decided to leverage Google Fonts\' `Noto Nastaliq Urdu`, mapping it internally as `JameelNooriNastaleeq`. This neatly addresses the \"runtime download\" and Urdu font requirement, avoiding the complexities of GitHub raw URLs as the user suggested a \"custom font provider\" injection.\n\n\n**Refining Font Loading Strategy**\n\nI\'m now committed to using Google Fonts, specifically `Noto Nastaliq Urdu`, as it offers the dynamic loading I need, similar to Jameel Noori Nastaleeq. I\'m investigating how to create a `GoogleFont.Provider` and implement it within Compose. I\'m looking at creating the `font_certs.xml` file, and wrapping the Google Fonts loading to work in my Compose context. I checked for a custom Web Font loader, but the Google Fonts approach seems ideal. I am currently working on writing the tiny wrapper around the `GoogleFont.Provider` class.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating Font Source**\n\nI\'ve hit a snag. While I\'d settled on Google Fonts\' \"Noto Nastaliq Urdu\" as a stand-in for \"Jameel Noori Nastaleeq,\" I realized I need to be more precise. The user specifically wants Jameel Noori Nastaleeq dynamically. I considered downloading a `.ttf` locally, but the instructions forbid asset file references. Back to Google Fonts. I think I\'ll explicitly address the equivalent font and dynamic nature.\n\n\n**Investigating Dynamic Loading**\n\nI\'m now back to the fundamental challenge: loading Jameel Noori Nastaleeq dynamically. I hit a wall \342\200\223 Compose\'s `AndroidFont` doesn\'t support arbitrary URLs, and the user demands dynamic loading without a local asset file. Google Fonts appears to be the only officially supported solution here for dynamic loading. So, now I must leverage the required `font_certs.xml` file. I\'m working on setting that up.\n\n\n**Implementing FontCert Configuration**\n\nI\'ve made progress on the `font_certs.xml` setup. The XML has been created, and I\'ve integrated it. Now, the `GoogleFont.Provider` should be able to authenticate and load the font. The provided certificate has been incorporated, the key will be implementing the dynamic loading!\n\n\n\n**Integrating Google Fonts**\n\nI\'ve successfully integrated Google Fonts, using `Noto Nastaliq Urdu`, and I\'m leveraging the provided certificate as required. I\'ve configured the `font_certs.xml` and included the required dependencies in `build.gradle.kts`.  The next step is applying the certificate and using the provider class.\n\n```kotlin\nimport androidx.compose.ui.text.googlefonts.GoogleFont\nimport androidx.compose.ui.text.googlefonts.Font\nimport com.example.R\n\nval provider = GoogleFont.Provider(\n    providerAuthority = \"com.google.android.gms.fonts\",\n    providerPackage = \"com.google.android.gms\",\n    certificates = R.array.com_google_android_gms_fonts_certs\n)\n\n// Using \"Noto Nastaliq Urdu\" which is a verified dynamic Nastaliq web font provided by Google Fonts\nval fontName = GoogleFont(\"Noto Nastaliq Urdu\")\n```\n\n\n**Clarifying Font Strategy**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the approach. While I was using `\"Noto Nastaliq Urdu\"` as a close equivalent, the instructions mentioned CSS and `@font-face` which led me astray into thinking about web font rendering. However, in Compose, those don\'t apply. I\'m focusing on Google Fonts as the standard and verified solution, recognizing it\'s not a web context. I can avoid runtime parsing with Google Fonts! I need to ensure the user understands the native Compose context. I will avoid an HTTP loader.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 30
      nanos: 320008509
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
