    thinking: "**Refining Navigation Strategy**\n\nI\'m focusing on standardizing navigation padding. The current hardcoded bottom padding in the `BottomNavBar` needs removal. Now I\'m working to correctly wrap the application in a Material 3 `Scaffold`. I\'m going to eliminate instances of individual screen `Scaffold` definitions.\n\n\n**Consolidating Navigation Structure**\n\nI\'ve made progress in simplifying the navigation setup. My current focus is hoisting the `BottomNavBar` to a single `Scaffold` within `KapasApp`, placing it in the `bottomBar` slot. This means I\'m removing the `BottomNavBar` from individual screen components like `HomeScreen` and others. It is intended to standardize the `Scaffold` use throughout the application.\n\n\n**Resolving Font Fallback**\n\nI\'m wrestling with font fallback strategies. My current train of thought centers on the best way to handle situations where the primary Google Font, \"Jameel Noori Nastaleeq,\" might not be available. I\'ve considered several approaches: adding a `FontFamily.Serif` fallback within the font definition, using the Google Font\'s built-in fallback mechanism, and defining the font with a local resource. However, each has limitations. The most practical solution appears to be constructing the `FontFamily` with a defined fallback list, even though the direct use of `FontFamily.Serif` within the constructor requires a small refactor.\n\n\n**Clarifying Font Fallback Strategy**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the font fallback mechanism. It seems `TextStyle` doesn\'t inherently support multiple `FontFamily` fallbacks; it takes a single `FontFamily`. My focus has shifted. My initial approach of defining `JameelNooriNastaleeq` with an integrated fallback within the `FontFamily` definition appears to align with the requirements. It should automatically fall back to the system default if the Google Font fails to load. I\'m exploring defining `val UrduTypography = TextStyle(fontFamily = JameelNooriNastaleeq)`.\n\n\n**Investigating Font Certificates**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `font_certs.xml` configuration, aiming to ensure it\'s correctly set up for the Google Font. The `FontFamily` constructor seems to only take `Font` objects, which makes a simple `Serif` fallback within the definition impossible. I\'m exploring the `GoogleFont` provider, as it takes a `certificates` array. I\'m going to recreate the `res/values/font_certs.xml` file with standard certificates. I\'m hoping this will properly address any potential issues with font loading.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 310253549
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
